\infty" 13194139533967 true] [17592186275176 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533967 true] [17592186275176 1111 #uuid "3ba531cc-1057-551f-9a86-2fc85ecd71b5" 13194139533967 true] [17592186275177 1034 "-\\infty" 13194139533967 true] [17592186275177 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533967 true] [17592186275177 1111 #uuid "1336ebc1-00e8-5060-99c4-9d61efe3b6fc" 13194139533967 true] [17592186275178 1017 #edb/ref 17592186275179 13194139533967 true] [17592186275178 1089 "q-339629" 13194139533967 true] [17592186275178 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533967 true] [17592186275178 1100 "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{6{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-2}{2{x}^{4}+3}$ is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533967 true] [17592186275178 1113 #uuid "4bc264da-3bc4-5561-9fa9-e0a37b51fa53" 13194139533967 true] [17592186275178 1145 "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-2}{2{x}^{4}+3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{6{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(6+\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(2+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6+0+0}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=3.$" 13194139533967 true] [17592186275179 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275180 13194139533967 true] [17592186275179 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275181 13194139533967 true] [17592186275179 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533967 true] [17592186275179 1041 "field-1" 13194139533967 true] [17592186275179 1104 #edb/ref 17592186275180 13194139533967 true] [17592186275179 1110 #uuid "9d88c5c2-353b-5e3c-9402-269cde16a082" 13194139533967 true] [17592186275180 1034 "3" 13194139533967 true] [17592186275180 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533967 true] [17592186275180 1111 #uuid "08f099b6-c4d1-5d21-ac2a-c02d25a98fce" 13194139533967 true] [17592186275181 1034 "2" 13194139533967 true] [17592186275181 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533967 true] [17592186275181 1111 #uuid "97633eac-78cb-5bea-bd54-88180978b6b6" 13194139533967 true] [17592186275182 1017 #edb/ref 17592186275183 13194139533967 true] [17592186275182 1089 "q-339641" 13194139533967 true] [17592186275182 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533967 true] [17592186275182 1100 "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ for $x>0$ with $f(x)=\\frac{|3-8x|}{4x+1}?$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote is $y=$\n\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533967 true] [17592186275182 1113 #uuid "e6f0ac3c-98f9-5ddd-b191-1756531965cc" 13194139533967 true] [17592186275182 1145 "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|3-8x|=8x-3.$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|3-8x|}{4x+1}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{8x-3}{4x+1}.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote for $x>0$ is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{8x-3}{4x+1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{8x}{x}-\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{4x}{x}+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(8-\\frac{3}{x})}{(4+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-0}{4+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$" 13194139533967 true] [17592186275183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275184 13194139533967 true] [17592186275183 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533967 true] [17592186275183 1041 "field-1" 13194139533967 true] [17592186275183 1104 #edb/ref 17592186275184 13194139533967 true] [17592186275183 1110 #uuid "94f42605-5fb1-5527-